<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=mesoamerican-long-count-calendar">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mesoamerican Long Count calendar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mesoamerican Long Count calendar counts the days elapsed since a fixed starting date in 3114 BCE, using units based on twenty. It appears on Epi-Olmec monuments such as Stela C at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tres-zapotes">Tres Zapotes</a> and was used most extensively by the Classic Maya to date royal inscriptions. The last known Long Count date of the Classic era, from 909 CE, was carved at Toniná.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mesoamerican-long-count-calendar.jpg" alt="Mesoamerican Long Count calendar" width="251" height="579" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maudslay · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:East_side_of_stela_C,_Quirigua.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Long Count served where the Calendar Round could not. A day named by both the 260-day tzolkʼin and the 365-day haabʼ recurs only after 18,980 days, about 52 years, so longer spans needed a running tally. The Long Count is a modified base-20 scheme in which only the second place from the right rolls over at 18, so that 0.0.1.0.0 stands for 360 days rather than 400. Dates were written with a dot for one, a bar for five and a shell glyph for zero, one of the earliest uses of the zero concept in history.</p><p>On Maya monuments a date is given once, at the head of an inscription, opening with the Introductory Series Initial Glyph and followed by the five digits, the Calendar Round and an optional supplementary series with lunar data such as the age of the Moon. The Maya held that the completion of 13 bʼakʼtuns marked the creation of the world of human beings, when three stones were set at First-Three-Stone-Place and the sky could be raised.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest contemporaneous Long Count inscription yet found is Stela 2 at Chiapa de Corzo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiapas">Chiapas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, with a date of 36 BCE that is not certain; the battered Stela 2 of Takalik Abaj in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> may be earlier. Several of the oldest inscriptions are in an Epi-Olmec rather than Maya style, and three of the sites lie several hundred kilometres west of the Maya homeland, which leads some researchers to believe that the calendar predates the Maya. The first unequivocally Maya example is Stela 29 at Tikal, with a date of 292 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>By the time the Spanish reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán Peninsula</a> the numbered Long Count was no longer in use; the Maya kept unnumbered kʼatuns and tuns in an abbreviated Short Count. Scholars match it to Western calendars through the Goodman–Martinez–Thompson correlation of 584,283 days, which places the starting date on 11 August 3114 BCE in the proleptic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregorian-calendar">Gregorian calendar</a>. Colonial records such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-landa">Diego de Landa</a>'s Relación de las cosas de Yucatán, lunar data in the inscriptions and carbon dates from wooden lintels at Tikal support it.</p><p>The end of the 13th bʼakʼtun on 21 December 2012 drew wide publicity, but Susan Milbrath of the Florida Museum of Natural History stated that there was no record that the Maya thought the world would end then, and inscriptions at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palenque">Palenque</a> project dates far beyond it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mesoamerican Long Count calendar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With it scribes kept several calendars at once: the 260-day sacred count, the 365-day year and the <b>Mesoamerican Long Count calendar</b>, which numbered the days from a starting point in 3114 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Mesoamerican Long Count calendar</b> counts the days elapsed since a mythical creation date corresponding to 11 August 3114 BCE in the proleptic Gregorian calendar.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-11">11 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A monument dated 909 bears the last known <b>Long Count</b> date of the Classic Maya, after which royal rule ended.</blockquote><a href="/state/tonina">Toniná</a></li><li><blockquote>Stela C carries a <b>Long Count</b> date equivalent to 32 BCE, and the site illuminates the transition from Olmec to later writing and calendar traditions.</blockquote><a href="/state/tres-zapotes">Tres Zapotes</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>36 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Chiapa de Corzo, Mexico</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The earliest date is uncertain: Takalik Abaj Stela 2 may be older, and the San Bartolo text of about 300 BCE may be the earliest Long Count notation.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=mesoamerican-long-count-calendar">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
